A useful family around sophistry is:

* Casuistry — overly clever moral reasoning used to justify a preferred action.
* Eristic — arguing to win rather than to discover truth.
* Rationalization — inventing reasons after the fact for what you already wanted or did.
* Motivated reasoning — unconsciously bending evaluation toward a desired conclusion.
* Equivocation — exploiting ambiguity by shifting a word’s meaning mid-argument.
* Special pleading — creating an exception for your own case without adequate justification.
* Cherry-picking — selecting only evidence that supports your position.
* Obfuscation — making something unnecessarily confusing so weaknesses are harder to see.
* Pedantry — over-focusing on technicalities while missing the substantive issue.
* Semantic laundering — changing the language around something to make it seem more acceptable.
* Motte-and-bailey — making a bold claim, retreating to a defensible weaker one when challenged, then returning to the bold claim afterward.
* Gish gallop — overwhelming someone with more claims than can reasonably be answered.
* Whataboutism — redirecting criticism toward someone else rather than addressing it.
* Straw man — replacing an opponent’s actual position with an easier one to attack.
* Apologetics — defense of a doctrine; not inherently dishonest, but can become sophistic when the conclusion is treated as untouchable.
* Plausible deniability — structuring words or actions so responsibility can later be disclaimed.
* Bullshitting, in the philosophical sense — speaking without much concern for whether the claim is true or false, provided it achieves the intended effect.

There’s also a useful three-way distinction:

deception manipulates the facts,
propaganda manipulates the audience,
sophistry manipulates the reasoning connecting the two.

And self-deception / rationalization is essentially sophistry turned inward.